      "t": "Tesla will stop making the Cybertruck in 2027",
      "d": "The decision to wind down the EV comes after just a few short years of underwhelming sales. Elon Musk said the company will use the factory space to make more Cybercabs.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/06/tesla-will-stop-making-the-cybertruck-in-2027/",
      "m": "11. 06. AM 11:50",
      "ts": 1793965810000,
      "fullText": "Tesla will stop making the Cybertruck next year, according to CEO Elon Musk, who said on X that the company will use the factory space to build more two-seater Cybercab robotaxis. The decision to wind down the futuristic, steel-clad EV comes after just a few short years of underwhelming sales. When Musk handed over the first few Cybertrucks in late 2023, he predicted Tesla would sell around 250,000 per year. It was supposed to be such a cornerstone of Tesla&#8217;s future that, before construction began, the company talked about its Texas factory as the &#8220;Cybertruck factory.&#8221; But Tesla has instead sold just tens of thousands &#8212; total &#8212; over the last three years. Tesla is killing off the Cybertruck at a moment when it is trying to become an AI- and robotics-focused company. A big part of that is its robotaxi ambitions. The company has spent the last two months putting Cybercabs on the streets in its hometown of Austin, Texas. With no major incidents to date, the company has said it plans to expand the Cybercabs operations to other cities, starting with Dallas later this month. (Tesla also operates robotaxi versions of its Model Y SUVs in Austin, Houston, and Dallas, as well as three cities in Florida.) This is the third product Tesla has discontinued in order to fuel those greater ambitions. Earlier this year, the company decided to end production of the Model S sedan and Model X SUV and instead use the factory space dedicated to those vehicles to make Optimus humanoid robots. First announced in 2019, the Cybertruck was a product that Musk had long obsessed over. &#8220;Probably my personal favorite for the next product is the pickup truck, and we are going to just do an amazing pickup truck,&#8221; he said on one of Tesla&#8217;s earnings calls that year. Musk promised the Cybertruck would be radical, inspired by the designs of cyberpunk sci-fi stories like &#8220;Blade Runner.&#8221; While that certainly bore out in the design, the company ove"
